x86_64-windows  refused before it reaches the question it asks, and by an older refusal than the one that would otherwise answer: `catches()` ends its handler with `return`, and on this target a handler is a funclet, so leaving one early means handing back the address to carry on at in the register a return value would travel in - which is not built yet. try-catch.notarget records the same refusal from the other side.

What this case measures would be refused here anyway. The region is a row in a sorted call-site list - a landing pad over the whole body whose action is terminate - and a Microsoft handler is a funclet and a state in the FH3 tables instead; msTryStatement is untouched. So this target keeps the half noexcept-terminates.cpp covers: a `throw` written inside a `noexcept` function calls abort there, and one arriving from a callee still propagates. Same lag rung 6.5 records for Windows exceptions generally.
